#!/bin/sh
# commit-msg — the gates that need to read the message being written.
#
# Why this hook exists at all
# ---------------------------
# `pre-commit` runs BEFORE git prepares a commit message. There is no
# COMMIT_EDITMSG on disk yet and `$1` does not exist, so a scanner run from
# there cannot see the message the developer is in the middle of writing.
#
# check-test-weakening.py offers an escape hatch for its own false positives:
#
#     test-weakening-justified: <path>:<kind> - <reason>
#
# read out of `base..HEAD` commit messages. From `pre-commit` that hatch is
# UNREACHABLE on a branch's first commit, because `base..HEAD` is then empty and
# the marker being written is invisible. The gate blocked with a remediation
# note describing a remedy that could not be applied; the only way through was
# `git commit --no-verify`, i.e. skipping every gate in the repo to get past one
# false positive. That is a fail-open pressure valve, and scripts/deny-no-verify.py
# exists precisely to refuse it. Observed 2026-07-30 on a real change: assertions
# extracted into a helper read to the scanner as coverage deleted (static count
# 62 -> 57) while the assertions actually evaluated rose 15 -> 25.
#
# So the scanner moved here, where `$1` IS the pending message. It blocks the
# same commit it blocked before — commit-msg runs before the commit object is
# written — and the acknowledgment it documents now works at the moment it is
# needed. Enforcement is unchanged; only reachability improved.
#
# Fail-closed contract, identical to .githooks/pre-commit: every non-zero exit
# blocks, exit 2 ("could not determine") blocks too, and a missing python3 or a
# missing scanner blocks rather than waving the commit through.
#
# `git commit --no-verify` skips this hook exactly as it skips pre-commit — the
# same single, explicit, already-recorded hole, not a new one.

set -u

MSG_FILE="${1:-}"
if [ -z "$MSG_FILE" ] || [ ! -f "$MSG_FILE" ]; then
    echo "commit-msg: no message file passed by git — refusing to certify this commit" >&2
    exit 1
fi

REPO="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)" || {
    echo "commit-msg: not inside a git work tree — refusing to certify this commit" >&2
    exit 1
}

if ! command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    cat >&2 <<'EOF'
commit-msg: python3 not found, so the test-weakening gate could not run.

"Could not check" is not "clean", so this commit is blocked rather than waved
through. Install python3, or bypass deliberately with `git commit --no-verify`.
EOF
    exit 1
fi

SCANNER="$REPO/scripts/check-test-weakening.py"
if [ ! -f "$SCANNER" ]; then
    echo "commit-msg: $SCANNER is missing — cannot certify, so this commit is blocked" >&2
    exit 1
fi

python3 "$SCANNER" --repo "$REPO" --pending-msg "$MSG_FILE"
status=$?
[ "$status" -eq 0 ] && exit 0

echo "" >&2
if [ "$status" -eq 2 ]; then
    echo "test-weakening: UNDETERMINED (exit 2) — the gate could not reach a verdict." >&2
    echo "  This blocks. An uninspected surface is not a clean surface; fix" >&2
    echo "  what stopped the scanner rather than loosening it to pass." >&2
else
    echo "test-weakening: blocked (exit $status)." >&2
fi

cat >&2 <<'EOF'

A red gate is a detection, not an obstacle — it found the defect at the cheapest
possible moment.

Restore the assertion, or, if the finding is genuinely wrong or the deleted test
is genuinely obsolete, acknowledge that ONE finding by adding a line to THIS
commit message:

  test-weakening-justified: <path>:<kind> - <reason>

Both the path and the kind must match the finding printed above, and a reason is
required — a bare marker acknowledges nothing on purpose. The line stays in the
commit message, where a reviewer meets it.
EOF
exit 1
